// The command-line reference: one file per subcommand of the heron binary.
include = [
  "./check.heron",
  "./fmt.heron",
  "./lint.heron",
  "./run.heron",
  "./generate.heron",
  "./lsp.heron",
  "./serve.heron",
  "./help.heron",
]

@lens(user, developer)
| The `heron` binary's subcommands, one reference each: usage, arguments, options, exit
| codes and examples. Extensions may register more.
|
| The commands share their conventions. Options come before the positional arguments and
| take one dash or two, except `heron run`'s, which take two. No option has a short form
| or reads an environment variable; the variables the extension host reads are listed
| with [the extension host](#system.extension_host). `check` and `lint` print diagnostics to standard output, one per
| line, with paths relative to the working folder; `run` and `generate`, which check the
| tree before they work, print its errors to standard error. Messages about the command
| itself go to standard error, prefixed with `heron ‹command›:`. Exit status 2 is a usage error, 1 a failure of
| what the command did, and 0 success.

scriptable: goal(measure = "Every command exits with a status a script can act on.") {{
  Scripts, CI jobs and agents drive Heron from the command line and know what happened from its exit status and output alone.
}}
